window.docContents[242].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Gabriel___s-theorem___-classification-of-quivers-of-finite-type-by-Dynkin-diagrams/#Chapter2___Theorem2___1___2":{"contents":"We will prove the following striking theorem, proved by P. Gabriel in early 1970s.We will prove this theorem when the field k is algebraically closed, but it is valid even without this assumption.\n\nTheorem 2.1.2. The finite type property of Q does not depend on the orientation of edges. The connected graphs that yield quivers of finite type are given by the following list:\n\n* A_n:\\circ \\text{---} \\circ \\text{---} \\circ \\text{-} \\cdots \\text{-} \\circ \\text{---} \\circ \\text{---} \\circ* D_n:\\begin{array}{ccccccccc}\n  \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ & \\text{-} & \\cdots & \\text{-} & \\circ \\\\\n  &&&& | \\\\\n  &&&& \\circ\n  \\end{array}* E_6:\\begin{array}{ccccccccc}\n  \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ \\\\\n  &&&& | \\\\\n  &&&& \\circ\n  \\end{array}* E_7:\\begin{array}{ccccccccccc}\n  \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ & \\text{---} & \\circ \\\\\n  &&&& | \\\\\n  &&&& \\circ\n  \\end{array}* E_8:o——o——o——o——o——o——o\n         |\n         o\n\n\nThe graphs listed in the theorem are called (simply laced) Dynkin diagrams. These graphs arise in a multitude of classification problems in mathematics, such as the classification of simple Lie algebras, singularities, platonic solids, reflection groups, etc. In fact, if we needed to make contact with an alien civilization and show them how sophisticated our civilization is, perhaps showing them Dynkin diagrams would be the best choice!\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.1. What is representation theory?\u0009Gabriel's theorem: classification of quivers of finite type by Dynkin diagrams","header":"Gabriel's theorem: classification of quivers of finite type by Dynkin diagrams","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Gabriel___s-theorem___-classification-of-quivers-of-finite-type-by-Dynkin-diagrams/#Chapter2___Theorem2___1___2"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-subrepresentation-existence/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Irreducible-subrepresentation-existence--Irreducible-subrepresentation-existence--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Over a field, no subtype of the displayed polynomial module is simple.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.3.15; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Irreducible subrepresentation existence\u0009Irreducible subrepresentation existence\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-subrepresentation-existence/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Irreducible-subrepresentation-existence--Irreducible-subrepresentation-existence--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Rad_LPAR_A_RPAR_-is-the-largest-nilpotent-two-sided-ideal/#Chapter3___Proposition3___5___3":{"contents":"Proposition 3.5.3. Let A be a finite dimensional algebra.\n\n(i) Let I be a nilpotent two-sided ideal in A; i.e., I^n = 0 for some n. Then I \\subset \\operatorname{Rad}(A).\n\n(ii) \\operatorname{Rad}(A) is a nilpotent ideal. Thus, \\operatorname{Rad}(A) is the largest nilpotent two-sided ideal in A.\nProof. (i) Let V be an irreducible representation of A. Let v \\in V. Then Iv \\subset V is a subrepresentation. If Iv \\neq 0, then Iv = V so there is x \\in I such that xv = v. Then x^n \\neq 0, a contradiction. Thus Iv = 0, so I acts by 0 in V and hence I \\subset \\operatorname{Rad}(A).\n\n(ii) Let 0 = A_0 \\subset A_1 \\subset \\cdots \\subset A_n = A be a filtration of the regular representation of A by subrepresentations such that A_{i+1}/A_i are irreducible. It exists by Lemma 3.4.2. Let x \\in \\operatorname{Rad}(A). Then x acts on A_{i+1}/A_i by zero, so x maps A_{i+1} to A_i. This implies that \\operatorname{Rad}(A)^n = 0, as desired. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.5. Finite dimensional algebras\u0009Rad(A) is the largest nilpotent two-sided ideal","header":"Rad(A) is the largest nilpotent two-sided ideal","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Rad_LPAR_A_RPAR_-is-the-largest-nilpotent-two-sided-ideal/#Chapter3___Proposition3___5___3"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___6___-Characters-of-representations/Linear-independence-of-characters_SEMI_-basis-in-semisimple-case/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___6___-Characters-of-representations--Linear-independence-of-characters_SEMI_-basis-in-semisimple-case--Linear-independence-of-characters_SEMI_-basis-in-semisimple-case--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The dual elements associated with a finite family of pairwise nonisomorphic simple modules are linearly independent.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.6.2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.7.1/Derived7; role=primary]\n\nFor a finite family of pairwise inequivalent finite-dimensional simple modules, the displayed\nfamily of algebra actions is surjective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Introduction_to_3.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.2.2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.2.2/Derived4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.5.4/Derived3; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.6.2/Derived2; role=primary]\n\nFor square matrices indexed by Fin d, the auxiliary submodule is the kernel of the trace\nlinear map.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.6.2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.6.2/Derived11; role=supporting]\n\nFor square matrices indexed by a finite type, the auxiliary submodule is the kernel of the\ntrace linear map.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.6.2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.6.2/Derived9; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.6. Characters of representations\u0009Linear independence of characters; basis in semisimple case\u0009Linear independence of characters; basis in semisimple case\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___6___-Characters-of-representations/Linear-independence-of-characters_SEMI_-basis-in-semisimple-case/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___6___-Characters-of-representations--Linear-independence-of-characters_SEMI_-basis-in-semisimple-case--Linear-independence-of-characters_SEMI_-basis-in-semisimple-case--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Factorization-of-the-Frobenius-determinant/#Chapter4___Theorem4___10___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.10. Frobenius determinant\u0009Factorization of the Frobenius determinant\u0009Factorization of the Frobenius determinant","header":"Formalization","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Factorization-of-the-Frobenius-determinant/#Chapter4___Theorem4___10___2___formalization"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Koszul-resolution-and-Hilbert-syzygies-theorem/#Chapter8___Problem8___2___10___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.2. Tor and Ext functors\u0009Koszul resolution and Hilbert syzygies theorem\u0009Koszul resolution and Hilbert syzygies theorem","header":"Formalization","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Koszul-resolution-and-Hilbert-syzygies-theorem/#Chapter8___Problem8___2___10___formalization"}});